.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Health and Social Care Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Governance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Council","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Environment and Regeneration Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Corporate and Inclusion Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-09","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Corporate and Inclusion Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Children Young People and Families Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Environment and Regeneration Policy and Performance Board","time":"6.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","time":"2.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://councillors.halton.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Halton Council faces \u00a372m five-year budget deficit despite \u00a329.8m savings plan","lead":"Halton Council's five-year financial strategy, presented to the Executive Board on 17 September, forecasts a cumulative budget deficit of \u00a372.567m over the period, even after \u00a329.8m in planned savings. The strategy document states this is \"clearly unsustainable\" and warns that without exceptional financial support (emergency borrowing from government), the chief finance officer must issue a Section 114 notice (a formal warning that freezes new spending), triggering an emergency council meeting within 21 days. The council has used emergency borrowing for three consecutive years: \u00a310m, \u00a314.7m, and \u00a333.873m. By 2031/32, servicing this emergency borrowing alone will cost \u00a327.504m annually\u201412.7% of total council resources. A CIPFA review found the financial position \"extremely fragile\" and concluded the council could not stabilise without continued emergency support.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Halton council faces \u00a373m five-year deficit despite \u00a330m savings plan","lead":"Halton council faces a cumulative budget deficit of \u00a372.6m over five years from 2027/28 onwards, according to its medium-term financial strategy (the council's financial plan for the next three to five years), even after applying \u00a329.8m of planned savings. Every year in the period will run a shortfall. The council plans to cover the gap using Exceptional Financial Support \u2014 emergency government funding for day-to-day running costs \u2014 which it has already drawn \u00a333.9m of in 2026/27. An independent review by CIPFA found the council's position \"extremely fragile\" and said stabilisation was unlikely without continued emergency support. If government does not approve that funding and the council cannot balance its budget, its Section 151 officer (the council's chief finance officer) will have to issue a Section 114 notice, a formal warning that freezes all new spending.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Halton Council faces \u00a372m five-year deficit despite savings plan, raising threat of spending freeze","lead":"Halton Council's Executive Board heard that the authority faces a cumulative budget shortfall of \u00a372.567m over five years, even after delivering a planned savings programme of \u00a329.8m. The council's medium-term financial strategy, covering 2027 to 2031, forecasts deficits in every year. The strategy depends on securing Exceptional Financial Support\u2014government permission to borrow or sell assets to cover day-to-day spending\u2014for a third consecutive year. The council has applied for \u00a333.873m in 2026/27 alone. A CIPFA review commissioned by the council found the financial position \"extremely fragile\" and concluded it is unlikely the council can stabilise its finances without continued support. If the government does not approve the application, the council's chief finance officer must issue a Section 114 notice, which freezes all new spending and requires an emergency meeting within 21 days to agree emergency cuts. The council's cost base is \u00a335m higher annually than comparable councils, forming the basis for the savings target.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","headline":"Equal pay claim risk identified from historic task-based payment arrangements, but no provision made; other councils have received claims.","detail":"The council has identified a risk that employment tribunals could bring equal pay claims based on historic working arrangements where employees were paid on task completion rather than hours worked, potentially creating higher effective hourly rates for some workers. The council is aware that other local authorities with similar practices have received claims. No cost has been identified in the forecast because the extent and makeup of future claims cannot be established.","category":"governance","matters":"An unquantified liability is a hidden risk. The council knows other councils have been exposed to this, so the risk is not speculative. If claims are received, they will create unexpected costs that cannot be accommodated in a council this tight on budget.","figures":"No provision; claims amount unknown","quote":"The Council has identified a possible risk in respect of equal pay challenges via Employment Tribunal. These claims may arise from historic working arrangements regarding start and finish times, where employees are paid on completion of tasks rather than hours worked. To date, no claims have been received by the Council, but it is aware that claims have been received by other local authorities who have similar working practices","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","headline":"Primary school surplus capacity in Runcorn; school closure consultation underway with risk of \u00a30.334m deficit costs and unmeasured workforce reduction costs.","detail":"Halton is consulting on identifying primary schools for potential closure, merger, or academisation due to sustained surplus capacity driven by declining birth rates and falling pupil numbers. Schools with deficit budgets at the point of closure will have those deficits fall to the General Fund. Current forecasts indicate potential closure costs of \u00a30.334m included in 2027/28. Additional workforce reduction costs from remodelling of school capacity are identified but not yet quantified.","category":"demand","matters":"School reorganisation creates both budget costs and service continuity risks. Workforce reduction costs are acknowledged but unquantified, meaning the forecast may be understated. Declining pupil numbers are a structural demand shift with ongoing implications.","figures":"\u00a30.334m estimated cost from school closure deficits; workforce reduction costs unquantified","quote":"There is a risk that some of the schools included within the consultation will have deficit positions on their budgets at the point of closure, merger or amalgamation, these deficits falling on the Council's General Fund. Based on current forecasts these costs could be as high as \u00a30.334m","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","headline":"Social care provider cost inflation assumed at 4% annually, versus 2% general inflation, driven by National Living Wage sensitivity (70% of care cost increases).","detail":"Adult social care contracts are assumed to inflate at 4% per year due to sensitivity to National Living Wage changes. ADASS estimates 70% of care cost increases are attributable to NLW rises and 30% to CPI. The Low Pay Commission forecasts NLW increases ranging 2.4-5.0% for 2027/28, creating a range of potential care inflation of 2.5-4.7%. The MTFS assumes the central case of 3.2%. Adult social care is the largest spending department and has 2.2% annual demand growth built in.","category":"demand","matters":"Social care is the highest-risk budget line. If NLW increases exceed 3.7% forecast or demand growth exceeds 2.2%, costs will spike without corresponding savings. The council has limited control over both variables. Workforce constraints in social care mean cost containment is difficult.","figures":"4% assumed care contract inflation; 70% attributed to NLW; NLW forecast range 2.4-5.0%; overall care inflation range 2.5-4.7%; 2.2% annual demand growth","quote":"The Association of Directors of Adult Social Services (ADASS) estimates that 70% of the inflationary increases to care packages are attributable to rises in the National Living Wage (NLW) and 30% are attributable to increase in CPI. The Low Pay Commission are currently forecasting the NLW to increase by 3.7% in 2027/28, with a range of between 2.4% - 5.0%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","headline":"Council tax base growth forecast 0.48% in 2026/27 and ~0.5% in 2027/28, versus government assumptions of 1.23%, creating \u00a31.1m+ annual Recovery Grant shortfall.","detail":"Government funding includes Recovery Grant based on assumed council tax base growth of 1.23% for 2026/27, but actual growth was only 0.48%, resulting in a \u00a30.54m shortfall in council tax income and loss of Recovery Grant funding. For 2027/28, the forecast assumes base growth of approximately 240 Band D properties (0.65%), generating a shortfall of \u00a31.1m compared to government's calculations, equating to loss of \u00a31.1m Recovery Grant.","category":"income","matters":"Council tax is the most controllable income source but base growth (new properties) is falling below assumptions. This creates windfall losses that multiply into Recovery Grant losses. The trend suggests the economic assumptions underpinning the budget are optimistic.","figures":"0.48% actual growth vs 1.23% government assumed in 2026/27; \u00a30.54m shortfall in 2026/27; \u00a31.1m forecast shortfall for 2027/28","quote":"For 2026/27, Government have assumed 1.23 % of growth to Halton's tax base, but actual growth was only 0.48%, equating to a shortfall in Council Tax income of approximately \u00a30.54m. Due to Council Tax being included in the transitional arrangements, this shortfall has a direct impact on the Recovery Grant funding received. The latest Council Tax base forecast for 2027/28 estimates a shortfall of approximately \u00a31.1m compared to Government's estimates","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Executive Board","paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","headline":"General Fund Reserves depleted to \u00a35.2m; strategy seeks to replenish to \u00a310.8m (5% of net budget) by 2031/32.","detail":"The council has moved from historically holding substantial reserves to support service delivery to a position where only \u00a35.186m remains in unallocated General Fund reserves. Of \u00a325.147m total earmarked reserves, all are committed to specific purposes. The strategy proposes adding \u00a32.8m replenishment in each of the final two years of the forecast (2030/31 and 2031/32) to reach a prudent level of 5% of net budget (\u00a310.8m by 2031/32).","category":"reserves","matters":"Depleted reserves mean the council cannot absorb unexpected costs, demand variations, or delays in savings delivery. The \u00a35.2m is insufficient to act as a buffer. Replenishment only occurs in years 4-5 of the forecast and requires delivering all savings first.","figures":"\u00a35.186m General Fund reserves; \u00a325.147m total earmarked reserves (all committed); \u00a310.8m target by 2031/32 (5% of \u00a3216.5m net budget)","quote":"the level of reserves is now such that using reserves in this way is no longer possible. There is an urgent need to start the process of replenishing reserves, although in the current climate and against the need to take on Exceptional Financial Support this makes replenishment difficult","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Halton UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":true,"headline":"Equal pay claim risk identified from historic task-based payment arrangements, but no provision made; other councils have received claims.","lead":"An unquantified liability is a hidden risk. The council knows other councils have been exposed to this, so the risk is not speculative. If claims are received, they will create unexpected costs that cannot be accommodated in a council this tight on budget.","also":["12 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Governance Board on 2026-09-30","n_findings":13,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"9 - MTFS 2027-2031 Executive Board","top_paper_url":"https://moderngov.halton.gov.uk/documents/s83255/83255.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":76,"news_drafts_total":3}}